From 87dc845f9fe68665218caaf96cfaf762d32414d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: albanread <albanread@googlemail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 22:17:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] HostFS: fixes from review -- no panic, no lost or resurrected
 data

- A file that changed on the host is no longer invalidated from lookups
  and directory reads (whose vnode may be dying: the file cache could be
  freed under us). They only mark the node stale; open, read and stat,
  which hold the vnode, drop the pages. When pages are mapped -- a program
  run from the share and rebuilt on the Mac -- file_cache_disable() fails
  and the node stays stale; this used to panic on the unbalanced
  file_cache_enable(). The refresh reads the host's size after its own
  write-back, and holds a new refreshLock throughout, so no space is
  reserved or truncated meanwhile.
- A per-node size lock: write-back holds it shared, size changes
  exclusively. Appends and growth reserve their range under it and only
  ever grow the size, so two writers extending a file no longer shrink
  each other's range (lost appends). Truncation asks the host first and
  then shrinks the cache, so a failed request loses nothing and no write
  from before the truncation reaches the host after it; negative sizes are
  refused.
- close() re-stamps the host's attributes only for the last writer and
  only if the size matches; otherwise the file is stale (a host change
  during a write session was absorbed before).
- get_vnode_name() copies the name under the volume lock; a rename onto
  the same node (a case-only rename on APFS) goes to the host instead of
  returning early; directory entries whose names Haiku cannot hold are
  skipped (their lookups forgotten) instead of ending the listing; a late
  failure in create() releases the handle and removes the file; reclaimed
  nodes release a handle they still hold; get_vnode() no longer looks up
  node IDs whose lookups were already returned; rename_attr() onto itself
  is a no-op.
- virtio_fs: any address that is not a kernel address goes through
  user_memcpy() (a NULL ioctl buffer faulted the kernel); a request whose
  completion is late drains its queue itself every 20 ms -- the bus
  manager re-enables a queue's interrupt without looking at the ring again,
  so a completion can come without one.

Verified: private_workspace/hostfs-hard.sh (running program rewritten on
the Mac, two appenders, truncate after a big write) three times, and
hostfs-test.sh.
